            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for central heating, then the Mandarin for to use little electricity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: shuā",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shuā — \"to scrub; to brush clean\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ shuā le yí ge xiǎoshí de guō — I spent an hour scrubbing the pot."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shuā is inherited from Middle Chinese srwaet (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'to wipe clean'; the dictionary also lists srjwet, glossed 'to sweep'. The level tone of shuā cannot be read off srwaet: words that once ended in t, as this one did, are spread over all four Mandarin tones."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying how it was."
            }
          ]
        },
